    description: `Physics problems never ask how fast the car goes in miles per hour — they demand meters per second, the SI unit where every formula from kinetic energy to momentum actually works. The bridge is 0.44704, an exact figure because the inch is defined as exactly 25.4 mm and the mile is exactly 1,609.344 meters. Multiply any mph value by it: 60 mph becomes 26.8224 m/s in the Meters per Second (m/s) box, and the km/h readout rides along at 96.5606 for good measure. Try 100 and you get 44.704 m/s, a number that makes triple-digit speeding tickets feel appropriately alarming in scientific notation. This page earns its keep in physics homework, engineering spec sheets, and sports science, where sprint speeds get analyzed in m/s and vehicle speeds get discussed in mph. The reverse direction divides m/s by 0.44704 — equivalently, multiply by 2.23694 — so a 10 m/s sprint is about 22.37 mph, faster than any human has ever run.`,
